WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.910
A Lucene or Elasticsearch index answers search questions written as JSON.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:10.448
In this video, you run one of those questions, give it a parameter,

00:00:10.448 --> 00:00:15.420
save it as a named query, and see where Elasticsearch differs from Lucene.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.238
You search an index by sending it a JSON query.

00:00:23.238 --> 00:00:28.096
When several templates and scripts each carry a copy, every change must be

00:00:28.096 --> 00:00:29.736
made in each of them.

00:00:29.736 --> 00:00:33.440
A saved query keeps one copy on the site, under a name.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:37.950
Lucene runs inside your site and needs no other service.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:41.832
Elasticsearch runs on a server that your site connects to.

00:00:41.832 --> 00:00:46.381
Each adds a query source to the Queries module, with the same fields:

00:00:46.381 --> 00:00:49.050
Index, Return Content Items and Query.

00:00:53.277 --> 00:00:57.875
In the admin menu, Search, Queries lists All Queries, and a Run page

00:00:57.875 --> 00:00:59.614
for each query source.

00:00:59.614 --> 00:01:01.727
Let's open Run Lucene Query.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.080
Choose an index that already exists on the site.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.400
The editor starts with a Match All query that asks for up to ten documents.

00:01:09.868 --> 00:01:14.226
Run Query lists the stored fields of each matching document in a table.

00:01:14.226 --> 00:01:18.698
Above the table, the page shows the number of matches and the time taken.

00:01:22.898 --> 00:01:28.400
This query finds published blog posts and sorts them by creation date, descending.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:34.278
A bool query combines two term conditions, and size limits how many documents come back.

00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:39.366
A query is a Liquid template, so a parameter can replace a value.

00:01:39.366 --> 00:01:44.438
Here limit sets size, and default supplies three when the caller passes none.

00:01:46.078 --> 00:01:50.162
On Run Lucene Query, enter the template and click Run Query.

00:01:50.162 --> 00:01:55.298
With no parameters, the default applies, and the table lists three documents.

00:01:55.749 --> 00:01:58.572
The Parameters box takes a JSON object.

00:01:58.572 --> 00:02:01.771
Enter a limit of two and run the query again:

00:02:01.771 --> 00:02:04.029
the table lists two documents.

00:02:08.239 --> 00:02:13.754
Save Query appears when the query returns documents and the Queries feature is enabled.

00:02:13.754 --> 00:02:18.559
Click it to open the Lucene query editor, with the query already filled in.

00:02:19.018 --> 00:02:23.145
Name is the technical name that callers use to run the query.

00:02:23.145 --> 00:02:27.518
It takes the letters A to Z and digits, and starts with a letter.

00:02:27.975 --> 00:02:31.960
Index is not carried over from the Run page, so choose Search again.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:36.537
Return Content Items makes the query return the content items that the matching

00:02:36.537 --> 00:02:40.145
documents refer to, instead of the fields of those documents.

00:02:40.598 --> 00:02:41.658
Click Save.

00:02:41.658 --> 00:02:45.709
The Queries list shows the query with a Lucene query badge.

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:51.568
Run opens Run Lucene Query with this template and index, and runs it with no parameters.

00:02:55.768 --> 00:03:00.865
Unchecked, a Lucene query returns the stored fields of each document, and an

00:03:00.865 --> 00:03:03.973
Elasticsearch query returns the source data.

00:03:03.973 --> 00:03:07.888
The Run pages always show documents, never content items.

00:03:08.638 --> 00:03:12.881
Checked, the query loads the content items by the version ID that each

00:03:12.881 --> 00:03:16.008
match carries, in the order of the search results.

00:03:16.008 --> 00:03:20.474
Elasticsearch reads that ID from the source data, so its index needs Store

00:03:20.474 --> 00:03:22.818
Source Data, which is on by default.

00:03:27.018 --> 00:03:30.294
Elasticsearch needs a connection to a server.

00:03:30.294 --> 00:03:36.395
Set Url and Ports in the OrchardCore, Search, Elasticsearch section of the configuration.

00:03:36.395 --> 00:03:41.918
The Run Elasticsearch Query menu entry appears only when Url is a valid address.

00:03:42.668 --> 00:03:45.576
The same question differs a little between the two.

00:03:45.576 --> 00:03:49.708
The Elasticsearch form of the query uses a boolean for Published, and gives

00:03:49.708 --> 00:03:51.238
the sort order directly.

00:03:52.856 --> 00:03:58.422
On Run Elasticsearch Query, choose an Elasticsearch index, enter the Elasticsearch form of

00:03:58.422 --> 00:04:00.567
the query, and click Run Query.

00:04:00.567 --> 00:04:03.756
The table shows the source data of each document.

00:04:04.232 --> 00:04:10.130
Save Query opens the Elasticsearch query editor, which has the same fields as the Lucene one.

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:11.382
Name the query.

00:04:11.835 --> 00:04:15.430
Choose the index, check Return Content Items, and save.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:20.321
The Queries list shows the Elasticsearch query next to the Lucene query, each

00:04:20.321 --> 00:04:22.325
with a badge for its source.

00:04:26.525 --> 00:04:30.378
A recipe creates the same query with the Queries step.

00:04:30.378 --> 00:04:36.415
Each entry gives the Source, Name, Index, Template and ReturnContentItems for the query.

00:04:37.165 --> 00:04:41.305
Among the recipes of the Blog theme are Blog - Lucene Query and

00:04:41.305 --> 00:04:43.285
Blog - Elasticsearch Query.

00:04:43.285 --> 00:04:49.405
Each replaces the RecentBlogPosts query with a search query, and reads its Template from a file.

00:04:50.155 --> 00:04:51.812
Schema is optional.

00:04:51.812 --> 00:04:56.518
The GraphQL API reads it to learn what the query returns, and for

00:04:56.518 --> 00:05:01.025
content items it names the type, such as ContentItem/BlogPost.

00:05:03.025 --> 00:05:07.021
A saved query gives a search question a name, a template and parameters,

00:05:07.021 --> 00:05:09.993
and a separate video covers running it by that name.

00:05:09.993 --> 00:05:11.325
Thanks for watching.

